A||||12||88formula||3.4 Pistons with 9.5 compression or higher?||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||08-21-2000||10:27 PM||||Where can I get hypereutectic pistons for the 93-95 Camaro pushrod engine? I read somewhere that I could use the 3.4 TDC pistons to get 9.7 compression. If this is true than what are the TDC pistons made of? I don't want cast pistons.<P>I will be running nitrous on this engine.<P>Thanks.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||08-21-2000||11:01 PM||||I would give Jeg's a call: 1-800-345-4545<BR>They are open 25hrs a day.<P>I am sitting here at the computer right now deciding on my 3.4L rebuild project, and I don't plan on using cast pistons either.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Xtreme Fiero's||08-21-2000||11:31 PM||||Use Foraged pistons NOT hypereutectic pistons on NOS hypereutectic can't take NOS at all. hypereutectic are great for Turbo and superchargers because they conduct heat well, but NOS WILL shatter them. Use foraged pistons for NOS. I'd check out Jeg's or Summit to find them||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||08-22-2000||05:12 PM||||Hypereutectic pistons are stronger than cast pistons so they should take a 50 shot of nitrous oxide with no problems. I brought this up with my machinist and he said that I could use hypereutectic pistons and run nitrous. He has over forty years experience building engines for people all over the country that have cost over $40,000 each. Forged pistons for the 3.4 motor cost $73 dollars a piece, which totals out to $438 for a set. Forged pistons are not worth the investment!<P>I will call the manufacturer about the hypereutectic pistons compatibility with nitrous.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||HYPTNOTiSE||08-22-2000||09:19 PM||||I think forged pistons are worth the investment, especially since you can make much more power with them. I can run at least 100hp more shot of nitrous than you can.  And the durability factor is they're too, plus you can set custom pistons up for total seal rings. I have venolia forged with total seals. The durability is there for the nitrous man. They have tapered wrist pins with snap ring locks as well. If you get forged pistons, dont cheeze out and get cheezy wrist pins.  I think these pistons and wrist pins set whoever bought them, they came with my engine  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   over 600 bucks.  I had to spend money on rings but oh well. <BR>If i had to start from scratch i'd get custom forged pistons, and i'd go with a longer rod as well and move the wrist pin hole in the piston higher up so that it's right next to the oil ring. Long stoke is cool!<P><BR>Mike LeCompte<P>------------------<BR>       Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ Nitrous<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/hypsig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||08-23-2000||05:15 PM||||HYPTNOTiSE, I appreciate your input but forged pistons are not worth buying unless you are going to increase the strength on the bottom end of your motor. Hypereutectic pistons should be strong enough for what I am doing. I only want to use a 50 shot of nitrous anyway. My Machinist has a dynamometer and he built a motor using the gapless rings and put it on a dynamometer he owns. He then replaced the gapless rings with molly rings (hand gapped them) and put the motor back on the dynamometer. He said there is no difference in horsepower or torque between the two and that the gapless rings are a gimmick. If gapless rings made more power than a standard set than why aren’t they used in NASCAR engines? <P>I found flat top pistons that are the pistons used in the TDC motor; they are the Federal Mogul H684CP and Silv-O-lite 10130958 pistons. I read somewhere that these pistons will give me a 9.7 to 1 compression ratio. Does anyone know this for sure?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 08-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||ThatRickGuy||08-23-2000||10:40 PM||||The thing about someone investing $40k in an engine is that you know the engine is going to be in some sort of pro cometition. And in most pro comps I know of, engines are not reused all that much, nascar gets what, like 3 runs out of an engine? NHRA, heck how many times can you get an engine to push you a 1/4 mile in under 10 seconds?!? So yeah, a hypereutectic piston may be able to handle a 50 hp shot of nitrous... but will it still be running later? and how many 50 hp hits could it take? I agree with using the hypereutectic pistons for Turbo and S/Cs, but for the extreme heat and preasure of nitrous, I'd say go forged, yeah, there more expencive, but you wouldn't have to worry about "will my engine shatter this time?" everytime you hit it.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-24-2000||11:21 AM||||You do have a reliable source of fuel for this thing lined up already I hope. Once you get over about 9.5:1 you likely won't be able to run pump gas anymore. Even if you can it's probly going to be really crabby about what brands.<P>You should probly consult Jeg's or someone before you buy the pistons. They can tell you what will work with nitrous. It's not just a problem of how much nitrous. It's an issue of how well the piston alloy can take being hit with a cryogenic when it's hot. Some pistons don't like nitrous at all becuase it can crash cool the face of the piston. They do the same thing a peice of hot glass does when you spray it with water. Nitrous sprays can be really cold when they enter the cylinder depending on where in the intake they are and the volume pumped in.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Shaun41178||08-24-2000||04:56 PM||||I don't think you will have any problems running a 50 shot of nitrous on hyper pistons.  I have been running a 70 shot and as high as a 90 shot on my stock cast 2.8 pistons for over 2 years now and they still haven't blown up or cracked or anything else.  in fact they still seal well as I have donea  compression test and all the cylinders were in the 150 range.<P>I plan on running a 100 shot with my 3.2 with hyper pistons and i don't think that there will be any problems with that setup either.  I do have plans for more fuel to accompany the nitrous too.<P>Make sure you do have the necessary fuel upgrades with nitrous cause even forged pistons will go south without enough fuel.<P>Hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>3.2 With nitrous<BR>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178||||reg||1
Z||000009||1FST2M6||08-24-2000||05:09 PM||||I've shattered a set of Hypereutectic pisons in a 402 in my old 79 Corvette. the very first nitrous install i had and it didn't last 3 seconds. the literature from NOS, Nitrous works and all the rest of them say if your running hyper- pistons not to use nitrous. but to use "a good forged piston" the aluminum and silicon mix will oxidize to fast in the extreme pressure and fall apart in about 2 revs. <BR> <A HREF="http://shop.barnesandnoble.com/booksearch/results.asp?WRD=how+to+nitrous&userid=66KQAZY4MF" TARGET=_blank>http://shop.barnesandnoble.com/booksearch/results.asp?WRD=how+to+nitrous&userid=66KQAZY4MF</A> <P>get this book.. it'll help you tune your car with nitrous and tell you why stuff fails like it does.. Nitrous doens't kill you car lack of fuel kills your car.. or Improper piston material. and i've never seen a set of total seal rings do their job correctly... we've had 2 lingenfelter 383 Camaros come in the shop all with ring problem and under 10k on the new motor... along with motors from K&G and JG engineering in California for older customers with 4cyl turbo and S/C cars... all the shop uses now is Arias slugs and federal mogul rings||||reg||1
Z||000010||88formula||08-24-2000||08:19 PM||||I have the Federal Mogul master catalog and in the catalog it states "Hypereutectic pistons are an excellent choice for street performance, for claimer oval track engines, and for mild bracket racing use. They will also work well in milder supercharged and NITROUS oxide applications."<P>The pistons that Nitrous works may have been referring to could be hypoeutectic - less than 12% silicon content, which are cheap junk. Hypereutecic pistons are superior to hypoeutectic pistons. Federal Mogul hypereutecic pistons have a 16.5% silicone content and are lighter than cast pistons. Hypereutecic pistons are about 30-40% stronger than cast pistons.<P>Now that I have read about hypereutectic pistons in Federal Moguls master catalog and my machinist says I can run nitrous with hypereutectic pistons I know there will be no problems as long as the air fuel mixture is not lean.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 08-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Xtreme Fiero's||08-24-2000||08:42 PM||||One more thing to think about  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>It seems to me well all agree that <BR>Foraged pistons are the strongest <BR>and that they can take a Turbo a S/C <BR>and/or a NO2 system. <P>we also agree that Hyper pistons are <BR>very good pistons to and that they <BR>take well to turbo/ Super charger <BR>systems. But no one seems to agree <BR>on the NO2 posibilties of Hyper <BR>pistons, but your mecanic how want's <BR>to sell you hyper pistons/ Rebild <BR>you blown motor and Fedaral Mogual <BR>who lso wants to sell you Hyper <BR>pistons. just think with the money <BR>you saved buying the hyper pistons <BR>instead of the Foraged pistons` <BR>you'll have enough ti rebuild it. <P>just my 2¢ worth  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||88formula||08-25-2000||06:00 PM||||I don't think Federal Mogul Corporation would lie about the compatibility of their pistons with nitrous oxide and get away with it. Like I said earlier the machinist and engine builder I deal with has over forty years experience building high performance engines for people all over the country. I think the hypereutectic pistons will be a good choice for what I am doing. For me forged pistons would be overkill and a waste of money.||||reg||1
